**Claim:** $\displaystyle \lim_{n\to\infty}\left[\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx\right]^{1/n} = \max_{x\in[a,b]} f(x).$

**Proof.** Let $M = \max_{x\in[a,b]} f(x)$. Since $f$ is continuous on the compact interval $[a,b]$, the maximum is attained; say $f(x_0)=M$ for some $x_0\in[a,b]$. Since $f$ is positive, $M>0$.

**Upper bound.** For every $x\in[a,b]$, $f(x)\le M$, hence $f(x)^n \le M^n$. Therefore
$$\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx \le \int_a^b M^n\,dx = M^n(b-a),$$
so
$$\left[\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx\right]^{1/n} \le M\,(b-a)^{1/n}.$$
Since $(b-a)^{1/n}\to 1$ as $n\to\infty$, the $\limsup$ is at most $M$.

**Lower bound.** Let $\varepsilon>0$. By continuity of $f$ at $x_0$, there exists $\delta>0$ such that $f(x)\ge M-\varepsilon$ for all $x\in I:=[a,b]\cap[x_0-\delta,x_0+\delta]$, and $I$ has positive length (call it $\ell>0$). Then
$$\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx \ge \int_I f(x)^n\,dx \ge \int_I (M-\varepsilon)^n\,dx = (M-\varepsilon)^n\,\ell.$$
Taking $n$-th roots:
$$\left[\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx\right]^{1/n} \ge (M-\varepsilon)\,\ell^{1/n}.$$
Since $\ell^{1/n}\to 1$, the $\liminf$ is at least $M-\varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon>0$ was arbitrary, the $\liminf$ is at least $M$.

**Conclusion.** Combining both bounds,
$$M \le \liminf_{n\to\infty}\left[\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx\right]^{1/n} \le \limsup_{n\to\infty}\left[\int_a^b f(x)^n\,dx\right]^{1/n} \le M,$$
so the limit exists and equals $M=\max_{x\in[a,b]}f(x)$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
